WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.101 --> 00:00:03.069
<i> ♪</i>

00:00:03.102 --> 00:00:07.240
"I think, therefore, I am."

00:00:07.273 --> 00:00:08.775
But am I?

00:00:08.808 --> 00:00:10.443
I think. Ha.

00:00:10.476 --> 00:00:14.247
A single microscopic brain cell
cannot think,

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.215
is not conscious,

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:17.483
but if you bring in
a few more brain cells,

00:00:17.516 --> 00:00:18.551
and a few more,
and connect them all,

00:00:18.584 --> 00:00:21.788
at a certain point,
the group itself<i> will </i>

00:00:21.821 --> 00:00:24.057
be able to think

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:25.792
and experience emotions

00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:27.794
and have opinions
and a personality

00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:30.630
and know that it exists.

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:32.398
How can such astonishing things

00:00:32.431 --> 00:00:35.268
be made from
such simple ingredients?

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:38.471
Well, answering that question
means learning not only<i> who</i>
we are,

00:00:38.504 --> 00:00:42.341
but, more importantly,
<i> how</i> we are.

00:00:42.374 --> 00:00:46.279
Today, using what
neuroscientists know so far,

00:00:46.312 --> 00:00:48.714
I am going to make my hometown

00:00:48.747 --> 00:00:51.617
function like a brain!

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:54.387
( all cheering, applauding )

00:00:57.623 --> 00:00:59.759
<i> ♪</i>

00:01:11.737 --> 00:01:14.740
A single brain cell is tiny,

00:01:14.773 --> 00:01:18.311
both in size and abilities.

00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:22.148
But when enough are together,
they can do amazing things

00:01:22.181 --> 00:01:25.318
like be aware of themselves.

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:28.688
When the collective power
of a group working together

00:01:28.721 --> 00:01:31.457
is greater than the sum
of their individual parts,

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.860
that is called "emergence."

00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:36.395
In a similar fashion,
we as individuals

00:01:36.428 --> 00:01:38.397
are connected to
the people around us.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:41.734
Those connections
form communities that,
when functioning properly,

00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:44.904
can work together
to accomplish amazing feats.

00:01:44.937 --> 00:01:48.574
A great example is
"wisdom of the crowds."

00:01:48.607 --> 00:01:51.577
Even if not a single person
in a crowd

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:54.147
knows the right answer
to a question,

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.417
collectively, they could all
somehow know the right answer.

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:01.154
In 1987, economist
Jack Treynor

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:03.923
conducted
the "Bean Jar" experiment.

00:02:03.956 --> 00:02:09.228
He asked 56 students
to guess the number
of jellybeans in a jar.

00:02:09.261 --> 00:02:11.464
Now, as you can probably guess,

00:02:11.497 --> 00:02:14.200
not a single one of them
guessed the right answer.

00:02:14.233 --> 00:02:18.437
But amazingly, when he took
the average of their guesses,

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:22.575
what he got was a number within
just 3% of the real answer.

00:02:22.608 --> 00:02:25.244
Now, some people
guessed way too high,

00:02:25.277 --> 00:02:27.547
but others guessed
way too<i> low,</i>

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:30.616
so all together,
their errors balanced out,

00:02:30.649 --> 00:02:33.319
and from a whole bunch
of wrong guesses,

00:02:33.352 --> 00:02:36.322
the true answer emerged.

00:02:36.355 --> 00:02:37.723
What else can a crowd do?

00:02:37.756 --> 00:02:39.458
If I got a bunch
of humans together

00:02:39.491 --> 00:02:42.695
and had each one of them
act like a brain cell,

00:02:42.728 --> 00:02:46.199
turning on or off in response
to the actions of other people,

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:48.534
could I make a neural network

00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:51.304
like the one in our brain?

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:53.506
And if I had enough people,

00:02:53.539 --> 00:02:56.876
could intelligence, emotions,

00:02:56.909 --> 00:02:58.945
a<i> mind,</i> emerge?

00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:01.514
<i> If I recruited
 every single person</i>

00:03:01.547 --> 00:03:03.549
<i> in the country of China</i>

00:03:03.582 --> 00:03:05.785
<i> and arranged them like neurons,</i>

00:03:05.818 --> 00:03:08.588
<i> would the result not only be
 a simple brain,</i>

00:03:08.621 --> 00:03:11.591
<i> but something that can think
 and feel</i>

00:03:11.624 --> 00:03:13.826
<i> and be aware of
 its own existence?</i>

00:03:13.859 --> 00:03:16.829
Well, this is the China Brain
thought experiment,

00:03:16.862 --> 00:03:20.633
first proposed by Lawrence Davis
and, later, Ned Block.

00:03:20.666 --> 00:03:24.904
It's never been done before
and, well, unfortunately,

00:03:24.937 --> 00:03:27.240
I don't have access
to everyone in China.

00:03:27.273 --> 00:03:29.375
I made some calls,
and like a lot of them are busy.

00:03:29.408 --> 00:03:32.612
But the first step is to see
what a crowd in real life

00:03:32.645 --> 00:03:33.613
could even do.

00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:35.514
This hasn't been done
successfully before,

00:03:35.547 --> 00:03:38.618
but I want to blow
a neural network

00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:40.519
up to the scale of a crowd.

00:03:40.552 --> 00:03:43.723
And what better crowd to use
than one made of the people

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:47.426
whose emergent properties
made me who I am today?

00:03:47.459 --> 00:03:50.696
That's right, I am going home
to Stilwell, Kansas.

00:03:50.729 --> 00:03:52.865
<i> ♪</i>

00:03:56.669 --> 00:03:58.537
<i> ♪</i>

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:00.473
( birds chirping )

00:04:02.341 --> 00:04:03.776 line:0%
Michael:
<i> For help designing the brain</i>

00:04:03.809 --> 00:04:04.944 line:0%
<i> we would make out of people,</i>

00:04:04.977 --> 00:04:06.846 line:0%
<i> I recruited Chris Eliasmith,</i>

00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:09.282 line:0%
<i> director of the Center
 for Theoretical Neuroscience</i>

00:04:09.315 --> 00:04:11.384
<i> at the University of Waterloo.</i>

00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:12.985 align:start position:0%
So Chris,
we're headed south,

00:04:13.018 --> 00:04:15.655 align:start position:0%
going down to
the heart of Stilwell,

00:04:15.688 --> 00:04:17.023 align:start size:78% position:22%
- where I grew up.
- Nice.

00:04:17.056 --> 00:04:19.625 align:start position:0%
We're going to do something
a little bit weird. Um.

00:04:19.658 --> 00:04:22.728 size:78% position:39%
I want to create a brain.

00:04:22.761 --> 00:04:25.898 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Right.
- OK? But with
  a crowd of people.

00:04:25.931 --> 00:04:28.067 align:start size:84% position:16%
It sounds like a challenge,
for sure.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:28.968 align:start position:0%
I looked into it,

00:04:29.001 --> 00:04:31.971 align:start position:0%
and I found that the roundworm
has a brain

00:04:32.004 --> 00:04:34.874 align:start position:0%
that's made up of only
300-some-odd neurons.

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:36.842 align:start size:88% position:13%
- That's right.
- We can get 300 people,

00:04:36.875 --> 00:04:39.845 align:start position:0%
and where better to get
these people to make a brain

00:04:39.878 --> 00:04:41.046
than my hometown of Stilwell?

00:04:44.016 --> 00:04:47.920 align:start position:0%
This was the community
that, in many ways,
made me who I am.

00:04:51.557 --> 00:04:53.059
Michael:
<i> This is all downtown Stilwell.</i>

00:04:53.092 --> 00:04:56.729 line:0%
<i> Some of my earliest memories
 are from here.</i>

00:04:58.664 --> 00:05:01.534 align:start position:0%
This used to be,
and maybe still is,
a feed store,

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:03.869 align:start position:0%
and they would have sno-cones
during the summer.

00:05:03.902 --> 00:05:08.074 align:start position:0%
It was the most awesome,
delicious thing ever.

00:05:08.107 --> 00:05:09.042 align:start position:0%
But as you can see,

00:05:09.075 --> 00:05:11.610 align:start position:0%
<i> a lot of corn
 is grown in Kansas,</i>

00:05:11.643 --> 00:05:15.081 align:start position:0%
<i> but around here, the main thing
 that I saw being grown</i>

00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:16.049 align:start position:0%
- was just sod.
- Oh, really?

00:05:16.082 --> 00:05:18.651 align:start position:0%
Yeah, There's a famous sod farm
around here

00:05:18.684 --> 00:05:20.820 align:start position:0%
whose slogan was
"High on grass."

00:05:20.853 --> 00:05:24.824 align:start size:97% position:3%
- ( Chris laughs )
- It was pretty...pretty edgy
 for the time.

00:05:26.859 --> 00:05:29.662 size:75% position:38%
OK, so back to the brain
that we're gonna make.

00:05:29.695 --> 00:05:31.797 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, building brains is
in my job description.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:33.799 align:start size:69% position:31%
I wrote a book called
<i> How to Build a Brain.</i>

00:05:33.832 --> 00:05:35.935 line:0%
Michael:
<i> Chris is known for
 is neural network,</i>

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:38.671 line:0%
<i> the Semantic Pointer
 Architecture Unified Network,</i>

00:05:38.704 --> 00:05:41.474
<i> or SPAUN, which is one of
 the world's most complex</i>

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:43.909
<i> computer simulations
 of the brain.</i>

00:05:43.942 --> 00:05:46.712
<i> It uses 6.6 million
 simulated neurons</i>

00:05:46.745 --> 00:05:49.548
<i> to perform functions
 like counting, reasoning,</i>

00:05:49.581 --> 00:05:51.384
<i> and image recognition.</i>

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:52.818
<i> SPAUN is cutting-edge,</i>

00:05:52.851 --> 00:05:54.920
<i> but neural networks
 are nothing new.</i>

00:05:54.953 --> 00:05:57.390
<i> The first was made
 by Dr. Frank Rosenblatt</i>

00:05:57.423 --> 00:06:00.493
<i> of Cornell University in 1957.</i>

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:02.828
<i> His network, called
 the Perceptron,</i>

00:06:02.861 --> 00:06:04.897
<i> was designed for
 image recognition,</i>

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:07.500
<i> and he hoped it would become
 capable of learning,</i>

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:08.734
<i> just like a brain.</i>

00:06:08.768 --> 00:06:10.870
<i> But the project was only
 partially successful,</i>

00:06:10.903 --> 00:06:14.006
<i> and after some controversy,
 fell by the wayside.</i>

00:06:14.039 --> 00:06:16.776
<i> It was only when researchers
 in the 1980s</i>

00:06:16.809 --> 00:06:18.744
<i> came back upon
 Dr. Rosenblatt's work,</i>

00:06:18.777 --> 00:06:20.713
<i> and as computing power
 increased,</i>

00:06:20.746 --> 00:06:22.882
<i> that the field of artificial
 neural networks</i>

00:06:22.915 --> 00:06:24.650
<i> came back to the mainstream.</i>

00:06:24.683 --> 00:06:26.752
<i> Today, it is alive and well.</i>

00:06:26.785 --> 00:06:30.189
<i> SPAUN, and even neural networks
 used in self-driving cars,</i>

00:06:30.222 --> 00:06:33.726
<i> are expanding the possibilities
 of computer learning.</i>

00:06:35.661 --> 00:06:38.464 align:start position:0% line:0%
If I want to make
a brain out of people,
where do I start?

00:06:38.497 --> 00:06:39.565
That's a good question.

00:06:39.598 --> 00:06:41.500
I think the first thing
we want to do is figure out

00:06:41.533 --> 00:06:42.868
what we want our brain to do.

00:06:42.901 --> 00:06:44.737 align:start position:0%
I would recommend
something like vision.

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:48.074 align:start size:56% position:44%
Vision. Let's make
this brain see.

00:06:49.608 --> 00:06:51.510
Michael:
<i> Before we can design
 the intricacies</i>

00:06:51.543 --> 00:06:52.711
<i> of the brain we're making,</i>

00:06:52.744 --> 00:06:55.481
<i> let's look at how
 visual processing works.</i>

00:06:55.514 --> 00:06:58.050
<i> Let's say we look at a cat.</i>

00:06:58.083 --> 00:07:00.586
<i> Light information
 from every point on the cat</i>

00:07:00.619 --> 00:07:02.188
<i> lands on the retina.</i>

00:07:02.221 --> 00:07:05.958
<i> This information gets sent
 to our visual cortex.</i>

00:07:05.991 --> 00:07:08.661
<i> The visual cortex 
 is structured in layers--</i>

00:07:08.694 --> 00:07:10.796
<i> V1 through V6.</i>

00:07:10.829 --> 00:07:12.898 line:0%
<i> Each of these layers
 are made up of neurons</i>

00:07:12.931 --> 00:07:14.767 line:0%
<i> activated by specific features,</i>

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:17.803 line:0%
<i> like lines, angles, and shapes.</i>

00:07:17.836 --> 00:07:19.205 line:0%
<i> The features that are detected</i>

00:07:19.238 --> 00:07:21.607 line:0%
<i> are sent to
 the infratemporal cortex</i>

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:23.776 line:0%
<i> which puts all the pieces
 of the image together,</i>

00:07:23.809 --> 00:07:25.945
<i> and we get our Eureka! moment</i>

00:07:25.978 --> 00:07:28.047
<i> where we recognize
 the object we're looking at,</i>

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.683
<i> what it means what feelings
 we have towards it.</i>

00:07:32.017 --> 00:07:33.752 align:start size:44% position:56%
I love cats.

00:07:33.785 --> 00:07:36.722 align:start size:72% position:28%
But what should we have
our brain recognize?

00:07:36.755 --> 00:07:38.257 align:start position:0%
We don't want
really high resolution images

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:40.226 align:start position:0%
or images that depend
on too much detail,

00:07:40.259 --> 00:07:42.161 align:start position:0%
- Ok.
- so things like letters
and digits.

00:07:42.194 --> 00:07:43.262 size:88% position:56%
Let's say we use digits. Ok?

00:07:43.295 --> 00:07:46.599 align:start size:69% position:31%
- Ok.
- I want to be the one
who draws a digit,

00:07:46.632 --> 00:07:50.002 align:start size:63% position:38%
and then you will be
on the output side.

00:07:50.035 --> 00:07:53.873 align:start size:91% position:9%
You should be able
to determine what I've drawn;

00:07:53.906 --> 00:07:56.842 align:start size:81% position:19%
not because I showed it
to someone and they
telephoned it back to you,

00:07:56.875 --> 00:08:00.079 align:start size:53% position:47%
but because
they processed it
intelligently.

00:08:00.112 --> 00:08:01.680 align:start position:0%
That's what we need
to figure out,

00:08:01.713 --> 00:08:03.616 align:start position:0%
how we're gonna
show an input to our people.

00:08:03.649 --> 00:08:05.150 align:start position:0%
So we should take
some small number of them

00:08:05.183 --> 00:08:07.286 align:start position:0%
and put them at the front,
as the retina,

00:08:07.319 --> 00:08:09.054 align:start position:0%
and really just show them each
a little bit of the image.

00:08:09.088 --> 00:08:13.893 align:start size:84% position:16%
So if, for instance,
we're able to put like
25 people in that kind
of front row,

00:08:13.926 --> 00:08:16.996 align:start size:84% position:16%
the "input" layer,
then whatever image we show

00:08:17.029 --> 00:08:19.765 align:start size:53% position:47%
should be made up
of 25 pixels.

00:08:19.798 --> 00:08:21.100 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Exactly. Right.
- Twenty-five pieces.

00:08:21.133 --> 00:08:23.836 align:start size:44% position:56%
I'm gonna draw
25 people.

00:08:23.869 --> 00:08:25.704 align:start size:44% position:56%
1, 2, 3, 4, 5,

00:08:25.737 --> 00:08:27.773 align:start size:47% position:53%
6, 7, 8, 9, 10,

00:08:27.806 --> 00:08:29.675 align:start size:59% position:41%
11, 12, 13, 14, 15,

00:08:29.708 --> 00:08:31.877 align:start size:59% position:41%
16, 17, 18, 19, 20,

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:34.647 align:start size:59% position:41%
21, 22, 23, 24, 25.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:35.781 align:start size:53% position:47%
See? I<i> can</i> count.

00:08:35.814 --> 00:08:38.284 size:84% position:58%
These are our retina cells,

00:08:38.317 --> 00:08:40.085 align:start size:63% position:38%
and each one is
an individual person

00:08:40.118 --> 00:08:42.721 align:start size:81% position:19%
that's literally standing,
like, in a field.

00:08:42.754 --> 00:08:44.156 align:start size:81% position:19%
What do they then do next?

00:08:44.189 --> 00:08:46.058 align:start position:0%
They merely need to indicate
whether or not

00:08:46.091 --> 00:08:47.726 size:75% position:38%
their cell is on or off.

00:08:47.759 --> 00:08:49.895 align:start position:0%
- All right.
- So, they should start firing.

00:08:49.928 --> 00:08:51.130 align:start position:0%
They should start spiking
like a neuron.

00:08:51.163 --> 00:08:52.932 align:start size:56% position:44%
What could they do
to indicate

00:08:52.965 --> 00:08:54.900 size:84% position:58%
that they're firing or not?

00:08:54.933 --> 00:08:56.802 size:88% position:44%
They could jump up and down,

00:08:56.835 --> 00:08:58.604 size:78% position:39%
they could wave a flag...

00:08:58.637 --> 00:09:00.172 align:start size:50% position:50%
OK, I like that.

00:09:00.205 --> 00:09:03.208
When Chris and I use words
like "firing" and "spiking,"

00:09:03.241 --> 00:09:05.644
we're talking about
how brain cells, neurons,

00:09:05.677 --> 00:09:06.912
talk to one another

00:09:06.945 --> 00:09:10.082
by sending an electric message
from one cell...

00:09:11.717 --> 00:09:12.551
to another.

00:09:12.585 --> 00:09:14.620
It's called
an "action potential,"

00:09:14.653 --> 00:09:17.122
and it travels down the axon
of the cell.

00:09:17.155 --> 00:09:19.692
When the ionic flow
into a brain cell

00:09:19.725 --> 00:09:20.826
reaches a certain threshold,

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:24.330
the cell will fire an electronic
message down its axon.

00:09:24.363 --> 00:09:27.132
So a neuron
can either be on or off.

00:09:27.165 --> 00:09:29.735
It's either firing
or it's not firing.

00:09:29.768 --> 00:09:32.237
What we need to find
is a way for a person

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:34.006
to be either on or off--

00:09:34.039 --> 00:09:37.309
raising a flag or their hand
should do the trick.

00:09:37.342 --> 00:09:39.244 line:0%
<i> To illustrate our visual input,</i>

00:09:39.277 --> 00:09:41.647 line:0%
<i> I will be drawing a number
 from 0 to 9</i>

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:45.217 line:0%
<i> onto a grid divided
 into 25 squares, or pixels.</i>

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:48.921 line:0%
<i> Now each person, or neuron,
 will receive one pixel.</i>

00:09:48.954 --> 00:09:51.090 line:0%
<i> If a neuron receives
 a pixel with writing on it,</i>

00:09:51.123 --> 00:09:52.891 line:0%
<i> it will fire.</i>

00:09:52.924 --> 00:09:55.861 line:0%
<i> The V1 layer identifies
 pixels in the retinal layer</i>

00:09:55.894 --> 00:09:58.330 line:0%
<i> that form particular lines
 in the number,</i>

00:09:58.363 --> 00:10:01.700 line:0%
<i> and the V2 layer identifies
 particular combinations</i>

00:10:01.733 --> 00:10:04.970 line:0%
<i> of lines from V1
 that form angles.</i>

00:10:05.003 --> 00:10:08.707 align:start position:0%
- What does V3 do?
- V3 is more sensitive to color.

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:10.108 align:start position:0%
We're only working with
black and white in this case.

00:10:10.142 --> 00:10:12.311
So you're saying we won't even
need to have a V3 in our brain?

00:10:12.344 --> 00:10:13.879
We're skipping V3 altogether.

00:10:13.912 --> 00:10:15.714 align:start size:66% position:34%
All right, sorry, V3.

00:10:15.747 --> 00:10:18.183 align:start size:81% position:19%
- So we're gonna go
 straight to V4?
- Yeah.

00:10:18.216 --> 00:10:19.818
Michael:
<i> V4 neurons will fire</i>

00:10:19.851 --> 00:10:21.987
<i> when their assigned
 combinations of angles</i>

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:23.155
<i> have been detected.</i>

00:10:23.188 --> 00:10:25.124
<i> At this point
 the basic shape of a number</i>

00:10:25.157 --> 00:10:26.892
<i> is beginning to take form.</i>

00:10:26.925 --> 00:10:29.862 align:start position:0%
And so actually the next one
is called IT.

00:10:29.895 --> 00:10:32.331 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Ooh!
- And that stands for
 infratemporal cortex.

00:10:32.364 --> 00:10:33.899 line:0%
Michael:
<i> Now don't worry.</i>

00:10:33.932 --> 00:10:35.868 line:0%
<i> We haven't forgotten V5 and V6.</i>

00:10:35.901 --> 00:10:37.736 line:0%
<i> They exist, they're responsible</i>

00:10:37.769 --> 00:10:40.239 line:0%
<i> for higher-level image
 processing in our brain.</i>

00:10:40.272 --> 00:10:43.308 line:0%
<i> But for our demonstration,
 we don't need them.</i>

00:10:43.341 --> 00:10:46.345 line:0%
<i> We do, however, need
 the infratemporal cortex,</i>

00:10:46.378 --> 00:10:48.947 line:0%
<i> which is the final layer needed
 for visual processing</i>

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:50.716 line:0%
<i> in the brain we're designing.</i>

00:10:50.749 --> 00:10:52.918 line:0%
<i> Our IT will consist
 of ten neurons</i>

00:10:52.951 --> 00:10:55.754 line:0%
<i> representing the numbers
 0 through 9.</i>

00:10:55.787 --> 00:10:57.956
<i> They will be looking at neurons
 in V4,</i>

00:10:57.989 --> 00:11:01.326
<i> and will only fire when their
 corresponding neurons fire.</i>

00:11:01.359 --> 00:11:04.329
<i> For example, if one
 or multiple V4 neurons</i>

00:11:04.362 --> 00:11:07.032
<i> representing the shapes
 of an 8 fire,</i>

00:11:07.065 --> 00:11:10.769 line:0%
<i> the IT neuron
 representing the 8
 will also fire.</i>

00:11:10.802 --> 00:11:13.372 line:0%
<i> Voila! We just recognized
 a number.</i>

00:11:13.405 --> 00:11:16.075 align:start size:75% position:25%
So what happens after
the infratemporal layer?

00:11:16.108 --> 00:11:18.477 align:start position:0%
So after that,
I think that's where I'll be.

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:22.314 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's gonna be me making a
decision about what digit
I think was actually shown at
the end.

00:11:22.347 --> 00:11:24.817 size:84% position:42%
So I think we're gonna need
a couple hundred people,

00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:27.486 align:start position:0%
so one question is,
where do you put
that many people?

00:11:27.519 --> 00:11:30.055 align:start size:94% position:6%
I would love to use
my high school football field.

00:11:30.088 --> 00:11:31.724 align:start position:0%
The question is,
is it gonna work?

00:11:31.757 --> 00:11:34.360 align:start position:0%
- I am hopeful right now.
- We got our work cut out for
us.

00:11:36.128 --> 00:11:37.896
Michael:<i> Knowing how
 we want to structure</i>

00:11:37.929 --> 00:11:39.932
<i> our neural network,
 it was now time for us</i>

00:11:39.965 --> 00:11:42.034
<i> to head to my high school
 football field</i>

00:11:42.067 --> 00:11:44.303
<i> where our "brain"
 will take form.</i>

00:11:44.336 --> 00:11:46.004
<i> I spent a lot of time here</i>

00:11:46.037 --> 00:11:48.440
<i> impressing the world
 with my body's prowess--</i>

00:11:48.473 --> 00:11:50.209
<i> at the clarinet.</i>

00:11:50.242 --> 00:11:52.111 align:start size:56% position:44%
OK, so Chris,
I brought you here

00:11:52.144 --> 00:11:53.946
because we need to talk about

00:11:53.979 --> 00:11:57.282 align:start size:97% position:3%
the actual logistics of getting
all these people together.

00:11:57.315 --> 00:11:59.017 align:start size:56% position:44%
So here's the plan
as I see it.

00:11:59.050 --> 00:12:02.221 align:start size:72% position:28%
Everyone is going to be
wearing a shirt

00:12:02.254 --> 00:12:04.857 align:start size:97% position:3%
that is a different color,
based on what layer they're in.

00:12:04.890 --> 00:12:07.860 align:start size:81% position:19%
We're also gonna give
everyone one of those, um,

00:12:07.893 --> 00:12:10.229 align:start size:78% position:22%
like, "I'm running
in a marathon" kind of...

00:12:10.262 --> 00:12:12.231 align:start size:88% position:13%
- The big bibs?
- Bibs. Thank you. Yes.

00:12:12.264 --> 00:12:13.399 align:start size:78% position:22%
I knew that too. You see,

00:12:13.432 --> 00:12:15.167 size:88% position:56%
I'm always checking, because
Chris is one of those nerds

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:18.137 align:start size:50% position:50%
who doesn't know
what us sportos
talk about.

00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:20.906 align:start size:69% position:31%
Anyway, the bibs
will give every person
an individual number,

00:12:20.939 --> 00:12:23.108 size:84% position:58%
so if something goes wrong,

00:12:23.141 --> 00:12:26.378 align:start size:59% position:41%
we can target
that one brain cell

00:12:26.411 --> 00:12:28.046 align:start size:81% position:19%
and say, "Are you damaged?
What do you need?"

00:12:28.079 --> 00:12:28.980 align:start position:0%
Take out the problem.

00:12:29.014 --> 00:12:31.116 align:start size:72% position:28%
OK, we're here
on this 40 yard line.

00:12:31.149 --> 00:12:34.019 align:start size:59% position:41%
I will be here.
This is gonna be
the input layer.

00:12:34.052 --> 00:12:37.489 align:start size:78% position:22%
The retinal cells will be
all in front of me,
all 25 of them.

00:12:37.522 --> 00:12:41.527 align:start size:75% position:25%
You are gonna be
way down in the end zone
on the output side.

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:44.096 align:start size:66% position:34%
And I'm gonna be
using the scoreboard.

00:12:44.129 --> 00:12:46.565 align:start size:66% position:34%
-Ok.
- So when you
make your prediction,

00:12:46.598 --> 00:12:49.134 align:start size:81% position:19%
based on what you think
the brain has figured out,

00:12:49.167 --> 00:12:50.368 align:start size:56% position:44%
we'll put that on
the Visitor's side

00:12:50.402 --> 00:12:55.207 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I'll reveal the Home number
as what I really wrote.

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:58.377 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Sounds good.
- So literally from here
 to that end zone

00:12:58.410 --> 00:13:00.379 align:start size:69% position:31%
is the amount of space
we're going to need

00:13:00.412 --> 00:13:02.247 size:88% position:56%
for these hundreds of people

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:04.116 align:start size:63% position:38%
to also have
the right eye lines.

00:13:04.149 --> 00:13:06.218
Just have to make sure that
communication lines are open,

00:13:06.251 --> 00:13:09.188 align:start position:0%
meaning that it's easy to see
whoever you have to
pay attention to.

00:13:09.221 --> 00:13:12.124
Michael:
<i> Our human brain will have
 a couple hundred people</i>

00:13:12.157 --> 00:13:14.026
<i> spread out in five layers</i>

00:13:14.059 --> 00:13:16.094
<i> across half a football field.</i>

00:13:16.127 --> 00:13:18.030
<i> Every single participant
 will be assigned</i>

00:13:18.063 --> 00:13:19.898
<i> to only react
 to certain neurons</i>

00:13:19.931 --> 00:13:21.834
<i> in the layer ahead of them.</i>

00:13:21.867 --> 00:13:24.903
<i> And it's complicated,
 so their positions on the field</i>

00:13:24.936 --> 00:13:27.372
<i> had to be carefully chosen
 so that every neuron</i>

00:13:27.405 --> 00:13:29.074
<i> has a clear line of sight</i>

00:13:29.107 --> 00:13:31.143
<i> to the neurons
 they are connected to.</i>

00:13:31.176 --> 00:13:33.111 align:start size:88% position:13%
In a way...

00:13:33.144 --> 00:13:35.314 align:start position:0%
something will be born
on this field tomorrow.

00:13:35.347 --> 00:13:36.882 align:start size:41% position:59%
( laughs )

00:13:36.915 --> 00:13:38.584 align:start size:81% position:19%
- We shall find out.
- We'll find out!

00:13:38.617 --> 00:13:40.452
All right.

00:13:46.625 --> 00:13:49.528
( crowd chattering )

00:13:51.897 --> 00:13:55.200
Michael:
<i> So what does it take
 to turn Stilwell into a brain?</i>

00:13:55.233 --> 00:13:59.004
<i> Well, seven tents, 550 chairs,</i>

00:13:59.037 --> 00:14:02.140
<i> twenty gallons of coffee,
 three hundred flags,</i>

00:14:02.173 --> 00:14:03.909
<i> t-shirt and hats,</i>

00:14:03.942 --> 00:14:06.245
<i> our drone operator Jeff,</i>

00:14:06.278 --> 00:14:07.479
<i> this cute little Gator,</i>

00:14:07.512 --> 00:14:09.014
<i> two hundred cinnamon rolls,</i>

00:14:09.047 --> 00:14:12.117
<i> and of course,
 our medic, Brian.</i>

00:14:12.150 --> 00:14:15.153
<i> Now all that's left
 is to pull this all off.</i>

00:14:15.186 --> 00:14:16.655
<i> A community is something
 that is bigger</i>

00:14:16.688 --> 00:14:18.257
<i> that the sum
 of all of its parts,</i>

00:14:18.290 --> 00:14:19.892
and so is a brain.

00:14:19.925 --> 00:14:22.594
Now, today, I'm feeling
pretty excited about

00:14:22.627 --> 00:14:24.529
the neural network
we're gonna build out of people,

00:14:24.562 --> 00:14:27.332
because there's a zero percent
chance of rain,

00:14:27.365 --> 00:14:31.103
but a 100% chance of<i> brain.</i>

00:14:31.136 --> 00:14:33.338
OK, we better get started.

00:14:33.371 --> 00:14:36.608
<i> The gates are open,
 and our neurons are filing in.</i>

00:14:36.641 --> 00:14:39.378
<i> First, they're all given
 color-coded t-shirts</i>

00:14:39.411 --> 00:14:42.581
<i> associated with the layer of
 the brain they will represent.</i>

00:14:42.614 --> 00:14:44.316 line:0%
Just want go in the center?

00:14:46.318 --> 00:14:48.320
Michael:
<i> And then they will
 take to the field</i>

00:14:48.353 --> 00:14:51.089
<i> to get in position.</i>

00:14:51.122 --> 00:14:54.259
Michael:
You all in the orange shirts
are the retina.

00:14:54.292 --> 00:14:57.529
Your job is to say,
"Is there writing on my square?"

00:14:57.562 --> 00:15:00.599
or "Not." If your square
has any writing

00:15:00.632 --> 00:15:03.702 align:start size:84% position:16%
or black marks on it,
raise your flag--
oh,<i> and</i> stand up.

00:15:03.735 --> 00:15:06.438
<i> Now I'm sure all you
 mega-brainiacs out there</i>

00:15:06.471 --> 00:15:09.174
<i> remember every detail about
 how this brain</i>

00:15:09.207 --> 00:15:10.509
<i> is going to recognize numbers.</i>

00:15:10.542 --> 00:15:13.645
<i> But just in case you don't,
 here's a refresher.</i>

00:15:13.678 --> 00:15:16.448
<i> I will draw a number
 on a 25-pixel grid,</i>

00:15:16.481 --> 00:15:18.383
<i> break the squares up,
 and hand them out</i>

00:15:18.416 --> 00:15:20.151
<i> to every person
 in the retina layer.</i>

00:15:20.185 --> 00:15:23.522
<i> The retinal neurons
 will only fire if they
 have writing on their pixel.</i>

00:15:23.555 --> 00:15:25.324
The people in the yellow shirts,

00:15:25.357 --> 00:15:27.960
you guys are V1.

00:15:27.993 --> 00:15:29.962
<i> Each V1 neuron will be watching</i>

00:15:29.995 --> 00:15:32.097
<i> three retinal neurons
 in front of them</i>

00:15:32.130 --> 00:15:34.032
<i> and fire only if all three</i>

00:15:34.065 --> 00:15:36.034
<i> of their assigned retinal
 neurons fire,</i>

00:15:36.067 --> 00:15:38.236
<i> revealing lines
 that make up the number.</i>

00:15:38.269 --> 00:15:39.504
You guys are V2.

00:15:39.537 --> 00:15:41.206
You're a bit more advanced.

00:15:41.239 --> 00:15:42.741
You're looking for combinations
of features

00:15:42.774 --> 00:15:44.443
that make, for instance, angles.

00:15:44.476 --> 00:15:47.679
<i> The V2 neurons will be watching
 the V1 layer,</i>

00:15:47.712 --> 00:15:50.682
<i> and fire only if
 their assigned V1 neurons fire,</i>

00:15:50.715 --> 00:15:52.384
<i> revealing angles.</i>

00:15:52.417 --> 00:15:55.687
<i> V4 neurons will be
 looking at V2 neurons.</i>

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:58.223
<i> Their firing reveals
 combinations of angles</i>

00:15:58.256 --> 00:15:59.725
<i> that begin to form the number.</i>

00:15:59.758 --> 00:16:01.493
Finally, the purple shirts.

00:16:01.526 --> 00:16:04.062
You all are
infratemporal cortex.

00:16:04.095 --> 00:16:05.497
Extremely important role,

00:16:05.530 --> 00:16:07.232
not more important
than the others, though.

00:16:07.265 --> 00:16:09.501
<i> Part of the IT's function
 is to inhibit</i>

00:16:09.534 --> 00:16:12.571
<i> incorrect results it receives
 from the V4 layer.</i>

00:16:12.604 --> 00:16:15.140 line:0%
<i> For example, if V4 neurons
 are indicating</i>

00:16:15.173 --> 00:16:17.342 line:0%
<i> both a 6 and an 8,</i>

00:16:17.375 --> 00:16:21.079
<i> an 8 will outrank a 6
 because an 8 has more features.</i>

00:16:21.112 --> 00:16:25.083 line:0%
<i> Chris will determine the number
 by interpreting the results
 from the IT layer.</i>

00:16:25.116 --> 00:16:27.386
<i> Got it? Good. Because
 it's happening now.</i>

00:16:28.386 --> 00:16:30.055
Michael (over loudspeaker):
All right!

00:16:30.088 --> 00:16:33.425
It is time for me
to draw my first numeral.

00:16:33.458 --> 00:16:35.260
Stand by.

00:16:42.167 --> 00:16:43.535
There it is.

00:16:43.568 --> 00:16:45.670
Now it's time to distribute
these pixels

00:16:45.703 --> 00:16:47.606
to the photoreceptors
in the retina.

00:16:47.639 --> 00:16:50.642
25, 24...

00:16:50.675 --> 00:16:52.544
19...

00:16:52.577 --> 00:16:53.712
13...

00:16:53.745 --> 00:16:54.813
All right.

00:16:54.846 --> 00:16:56.415
I have distributed

00:16:56.448 --> 00:16:58.717
the input to the retina layer.

00:17:00.618 --> 00:17:01.753
Is everyone ready?

00:17:01.786 --> 00:17:04.156
( all cheering, applauding )

00:17:04.189 --> 00:17:07.326
Three, two,
one, think!

00:17:09.627 --> 00:17:11.830
<i> And they're off.
 The retina layer has fired,</i>

00:17:11.863 --> 00:17:13.365
<i> passing off signals to V1.</i>

00:17:13.398 --> 00:17:15.534
<i> V2 sees V1 firing,
 and also fires,</i>

00:17:15.567 --> 00:17:17.402
<i> cuing V4 and IT.</i>

00:17:17.435 --> 00:17:19.171
<i> There's a lot of flags
 on the play, folks.</i>

00:17:19.204 --> 00:17:21.106
Look at all this processing.

00:17:21.139 --> 00:17:23.108
Good work, stay up,

00:17:23.141 --> 00:17:24.509
I'm now walking back to Chris

00:17:24.542 --> 00:17:27.813
where he will tell me
what you guys have processed.

00:17:27.846 --> 00:17:29.848
All right, Chris,
it actually happened

00:17:29.881 --> 00:17:31.316 size:81% position:41%
way faster than I thought.

00:17:31.349 --> 00:17:33.085 align:start position:0%
It took me forever
to get over here.

00:17:33.118 --> 00:17:34.553 align:start position:0%
They were already done
"thinking."

00:17:34.586 --> 00:17:36.121 align:start size:78% position:22%
It was really interesting
to watch.

00:17:36.154 --> 00:17:37.689 align:start size:94% position:6%
We had a little bit of noise
in the system, for sure,

00:17:37.722 --> 00:17:40.425 size:88% position:56%
because we actually kind of
have two answers at the end.

00:17:40.458 --> 00:17:42.794 align:start size:78% position:22%
So I'm going to be doing
something that brains do,

00:17:42.827 --> 00:17:45.664 align:start size:84% position:16%
which is kind of
make a guess sometimes
based on the best evidence.

00:17:45.697 --> 00:17:47.499
Michael:
All right, Chris.

00:17:47.532 --> 00:17:51.403
What numeral
do you think I drew?

00:17:51.436 --> 00:17:54.172
Chris:
I think you drew a 3.

00:17:54.205 --> 00:17:55.808 align:start position:0%
Let's get that up
on the Visitor's scoreboard.

00:17:57.709 --> 00:18:01.379 size:84% position:42%
The numeral I truly drew...

00:18:01.412 --> 00:18:02.481 align:start size:88% position:13%
was a 3!

00:18:02.514 --> 00:18:06.151
( all cheering, applauding )

00:18:06.184 --> 00:18:07.319
Chris:
Nice work.

00:18:07.352 --> 00:18:09.287 align:start position:0%
There was some noise
in the system.

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:11.389 align:start position:0%
I think we can perfect this
a little bit,

00:18:11.422 --> 00:18:13.191 align:start position:0%
because it wasn't
a confident 3,

00:18:13.224 --> 00:18:14.559 align:start position:0%
but Chris did still
get it right.

00:18:14.592 --> 00:18:16.628 align:start position:0%
And by "Chris,"
I mean all of you.

00:18:16.661 --> 00:18:20.232
Michael:
<i> We gave each of our IT neurons
 a clear tube and plastic balls</i>

00:18:20.265 --> 00:18:22.200
<i> to make their job easier.</i>

00:18:22.233 --> 00:18:24.469
<i> Every time they see one of the
 neurons they're watching fire,</i>

00:18:24.502 --> 00:18:26.505
<i> they put a ball in their tube.</i>

00:18:26.538 --> 00:18:28.273
<i> If a neuron
 they're inhibited by</i>

00:18:28.306 --> 00:18:29.641
<i> has more balls than they do,</i>

00:18:29.674 --> 00:18:30.909
<i> they stop firing.</i>

00:18:30.942 --> 00:18:32.611 align:start position:0%
Michael:
Our model had a mistake.

00:18:32.644 --> 00:18:35.380 align:start position:0%
You should have been
inhibited by 254,

00:18:35.413 --> 00:18:37.749
meaning that if 254 is firing,

00:18:37.782 --> 00:18:39.518 size:88% position:44%
and puts a ball in the tube,

00:18:39.551 --> 00:18:41.153 align:start size:81% position:19%
- you just sit down.
- OK.

00:18:41.186 --> 00:18:43.788 align:start position:0%
But we didn't have 254
written down into your code.

00:18:43.821 --> 00:18:45.223 size:88% position:44%
I'm gonna do that right now.

00:18:45.256 --> 00:18:46.658
<i> With that kink worked out,</i>

00:18:46.691 --> 00:18:48.560
<i> it was time to try again.</i>

00:18:53.665 --> 00:18:57.469 align:start size:50% position:50%
Take that one.
Thank you.
24...

00:18:57.502 --> 00:19:00.505
Michael:
Three, two, one...

00:19:00.538 --> 00:19:01.707
go!

00:19:04.842 --> 00:19:07.445
Michael:
Ooh. There's not much happening

00:19:07.478 --> 00:19:10.315
in V2 and V4.

00:19:10.348 --> 00:19:13.318
Or IT, for that matter.

00:19:13.351 --> 00:19:15.720
Looks like our brain died.

00:19:15.753 --> 00:19:17.822
Michael:
<i> Something definitely
 went wrong.</i>

00:19:17.855 --> 00:19:19.558
<i> The processing stopped in V2.</i>

00:19:19.591 --> 00:19:22.427 size:88% position:44%
So, I think our brain broke.

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:25.664 size:78% position:39%
Not a single person in V4

00:19:25.697 --> 00:19:28.366 size:84% position:42%
or the infratemporal cortex

00:19:28.399 --> 00:19:29.901 align:start position:0%
has been activated.

00:19:29.934 --> 00:19:32.237 align:start size:84% position:16%
Chris: Seems like something
very strange happened

00:19:32.270 --> 00:19:35.373 align:start size:69% position:31%
when the light
went through the lens
and got to the retina.

00:19:35.406 --> 00:19:38.777 align:start position:0%
OK, but what you really mean to
say is that I handed the pixels
out to the wrong neurons.

00:19:38.810 --> 00:19:40.812 align:start size:97% position:3%
- It's lookin' that way.
- Wow, who would have thought

00:19:40.845 --> 00:19:42.714 align:start position:0%
that the worst-working
part of the machine

00:19:42.747 --> 00:19:44.549 align:start position:0%
would be the actual people
who get to be people

00:19:44.582 --> 00:19:46.551 align:start size:84% position:16%
- and not brain cells?
- I know, right?

00:19:46.584 --> 00:19:49.921 align:start position:0%
OK, I think
I'm gonna do an 8 this time.

00:19:49.954 --> 00:19:54.226 align:start position:0%
And I'm gonna put it
kind of up in this corner,

00:19:54.259 --> 00:19:56.695 align:start position:0%
or along that side.

00:19:56.728 --> 00:19:58.697 align:start position:0%
This is pretty weird,
it's not centered,

00:19:58.730 --> 00:20:00.632 align:start position:0%
it's not filling up
the whole space.

00:20:00.665 --> 00:20:03.702 align:start position:0%
Let's see if our brain
can recognize it.

00:20:03.735 --> 00:20:05.303
<i> Each pixel of our image
 needs to go</i>

00:20:05.336 --> 00:20:07.606
<i> to a particular retinal neuron.</i>

00:20:07.639 --> 00:20:09.341
<i> To make sure I didn't
 mess it up again,</i>

00:20:09.374 --> 00:20:11.309
<i> we put numbers
 on the back of each one.</i>

00:20:11.342 --> 00:20:12.644
Is everyone ready?

00:20:12.677 --> 00:20:14.613 align:start size:75% position:25%
- ( all cheer )
- OK.

00:20:14.646 --> 00:20:17.950
Three, two, one, go!

00:20:19.350 --> 00:20:21.453 align:start size:91% position:9%
Michael:
Oh, yeah.

00:20:21.486 --> 00:20:24.389 align:start size:59% position:41%
Whoa!
That was<i> fast.</i>

00:20:24.422 --> 00:20:26.291
Michael:
<i> Blink, and you'll miss it,
 so let's take a break,</i>

00:20:26.324 --> 00:20:29.394
<i> because this is the Mind Field
 Play of the Game.</i>

00:20:29.427 --> 00:20:31.763
<i> The 8 I drew contained
 13 pixels,</i>

00:20:31.796 --> 00:20:35.634 line:0%
<i> and bam! the 13 retinal cells
 connected to those locations
 are firing.</i>

00:20:35.667 --> 00:20:37.669 line:0%
<i> Now, that's what I call
 a sensation.</i>

00:20:37.702 --> 00:20:39.537 line:0%
<i> V1 reads the formation
 perfectly.</i>

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:41.006 line:0%
<i> They don't even know it,
 but each one firing</i>

00:20:41.039 --> 00:20:42.540
<i> means a horizontal, vertical,</i>

00:20:42.573 --> 00:20:44.542
<i> or diagonal line
 has been caught.</i>

00:20:44.575 --> 00:20:46.044 line:0%
<i> Now look at neuron 40's speed.</i>

00:20:46.077 --> 00:20:47.846 line:0%
<i> It's sensitive only to
 a horizontal line</i>

00:20:47.879 --> 00:20:49.681 line:0%
<i> low and to the right,
 which my 8 had.</i>

00:20:49.714 --> 00:20:53.285 line:0%
<i> If retinal cells 23, 24, and 25
 all fire together,</i>

00:20:53.318 --> 00:20:56.021 line:0%
<i> such a line has been sensed,
 and watch this--boom!</i>

00:20:56.054 --> 00:20:57.922 line:0%
<i> Champion reflexes there,
 folks.</i>

00:20:57.955 --> 00:21:00.992 line:0%
<i> If the V1 neurons
 a V2 player is watching, fire,</i>

00:21:01.025 --> 00:21:03.662 line:0%
<i> they stand, and that means
 that the lines V1 caught</i>

00:21:03.695 --> 00:21:05.397 line:0%
<i> made some corner angle.</i>

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:07.966
<i> Standing V4 neurons are shapes
 made by those corners,</i>

00:21:07.999 --> 00:21:09.701
<i> but it all comes down to IT.</i>

00:21:09.734 --> 00:21:10.835
<i> A bunch of them fire.</i>

00:21:10.869 --> 00:21:12.737
<i> Lots of numbers contain
 the shapes I drew,</i>

00:21:12.770 --> 00:21:14.306
<i> but there can only be
 one MVP,</i>

00:21:14.339 --> 00:21:15.974
<i> and, dang,
 look at this teamwork!</i>

00:21:16.007 --> 00:21:17.475
<i> 3 is inhibited by 8,</i>

00:21:17.508 --> 00:21:18.910
<i> 0 is inhibited by 8.</i>

00:21:18.943 --> 00:21:20.478
<i> If 8 is getting
 this much activity,</i>

00:21:20.511 --> 00:21:22.347
<i> sit down
 and let that neuron score!</i>

00:21:22.380 --> 00:21:24.883
<i> This, my friends, is what
 we in the cognition sports biz</i>

00:21:24.916 --> 00:21:28.720
<i> call...a gr8 play.</i>

00:21:28.753 --> 00:21:30.755
Chris (over loudspeaker:
The purple people neurons

00:21:30.788 --> 00:21:32.791
make me think that
you wrote an 8.

00:21:32.824 --> 00:21:35.994 align:start position:0% line:0%
An 8? Let's put an 8
up on the scoreboard.

00:21:36.027 --> 00:21:40.065 align:start position:0%
And as it turns out,
the numeral that I<i> did</i> write...

00:21:40.098 --> 00:21:41.099 align:start size:84% position:16%
was an 8!

00:21:41.132 --> 00:21:43.902
( all cheering, applauding )

00:21:43.935 --> 00:21:46.538 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Nice job!
- That was good.

00:21:46.571 --> 00:21:49.507
Michael:
<i> This was our first
 definitive success.</i>

00:21:49.540 --> 00:21:52.377
<i> Now it's time to really
 put the system to the test.</i>

00:21:52.410 --> 00:21:53.945 line:0%
So I'm gonna draw a 1,

00:21:53.978 --> 00:21:56.581 line:0%
but then I'm gonna add
a line down here

00:21:56.614 --> 00:22:00.385 line:0%
and I'm gonna do
a dot right there.

00:22:00.418 --> 00:22:03.421 line:0%
And we're gonna see if
that noise trips up our brain.

00:22:03.454 --> 00:22:06.992
Three, two, one, go!

00:22:09.761 --> 00:22:13.098 align:start size:59% position:41%
My guess is a 1.

00:22:13.131 --> 00:22:16.000 align:start position:0%
The number that I drew was,
in fact, a 1.

00:22:16.033 --> 00:22:17.635
( cheers, applause )

00:22:17.668 --> 00:22:20.572 align:start size:66% position:34% line:0%
7...and I'm gonna put
a line through it.

00:22:20.605 --> 00:22:21.973
Go!

00:22:23.708 --> 00:22:25.143
Oh, yeah.

00:22:25.176 --> 00:22:27.579
Man, these people are<i> good.</i>

00:22:27.612 --> 00:22:30.615
Chris:
The brain thinks
it's just saw a 7.

00:22:30.648 --> 00:22:32.550 align:start size:88% position:13%
I wrote a 7.

00:22:32.583 --> 00:22:34.552 align:start size:84% position:16%
- ( cheers, applause )
- Nice work.

00:22:34.585 --> 00:22:36.488 align:start size:84% position:16%
Nice work.

00:22:36.521 --> 00:22:38.656
Michael:
<i> With two successful
 results in a row,</i>

00:22:38.689 --> 00:22:40.759
<i> for the last test
 I want to see what will happen</i>

00:22:40.792 --> 00:22:42.861
<i> if I really
 mess with our brain.</i>

00:22:42.894 --> 00:22:45.530
I'm not even going to draw
a number;

00:22:45.563 --> 00:22:50.402
instead, I'm going to
fill in every single cell.

00:22:50.435 --> 00:22:54.672 line:0%
This means that
every single neuron
in the retina will fire,

00:22:54.705 --> 00:22:57.041 line:0%
will stand up and wave.
Let's see what happens.

00:22:57.074 --> 00:22:59.477 line:0%
My prediction, of course,
it's gonna look like an 8,

00:22:59.510 --> 00:23:02.748
because an 8 is a numeral that
fills in a lot of the cells.

00:23:04.148 --> 00:23:05.617
Are you all ready?

00:23:05.650 --> 00:23:07.652 align:start size:75% position:25%
- ( all cheer )
- Good.

00:23:07.685 --> 00:23:09.521 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Chris, are you ready?
- Ready!

00:23:09.554 --> 00:23:10.822
Michael:
All right, I'm ready.

00:23:10.855 --> 00:23:14.025
Three, two, one, go!

00:23:17.061 --> 00:23:17.862
Chris:
What?!

00:23:17.895 --> 00:23:19.598
( laughing )

00:23:21.132 --> 00:23:22.734 align:start size:88% position:13%
- ( all cheering )
- Michael: Look at that.

00:23:26.003 --> 00:23:28.606
- Michael: Holy cow!
- ( Chris laughs )

00:23:28.639 --> 00:23:30.742 align:start size:91% position:9%
It looks to me like you
essentially opened up the eye

00:23:30.775 --> 00:23:32.510 size:53% position:73%
and shone a laser
right into it.

00:23:32.543 --> 00:23:35.580 align:start position:0%
Right, yeah.
So what I did is,
I didn't even draw a numeral.

00:23:35.613 --> 00:23:38.650 align:start position:0%
I just scribbled
all over the whole thing,
I filled in every single cell.

00:23:38.683 --> 00:23:41.186 align:start size:88% position:13%
- So what does the brain
 think that I drew?
- The 8.

00:23:41.219 --> 00:23:43.755 align:start position:0%
I had no idea the inhibition
would work that well.

00:23:43.788 --> 00:23:46.658 align:start position:0%
We were able to single
all of that mess

00:23:46.691 --> 00:23:48.059 size:78% position:39%
down into just one guess,

00:23:48.092 --> 00:23:50.128 align:start position:0%
and it really was
the smartest guess.

00:23:50.161 --> 00:23:52.664 align:start size:81% position:19%
Right, it was the one with
the most features in it.

00:23:52.697 --> 00:23:55.500
Michael:
Congratulations to
the entire infratemporal cortex,

00:23:55.533 --> 00:23:58.803
V4, V2, V1, retina.

00:23:58.836 --> 00:24:01.105 align:start position:0%
You guys have been amazing.
Great work.

00:24:01.138 --> 00:24:03.741
( cheers, applause )

00:24:03.774 --> 00:24:05.844 line:0%
Today, I was a neuron.

00:24:05.877 --> 00:24:07.111 align:start size:50% position:50% line:0%
My favorite part
about the brain

00:24:07.144 --> 00:24:08.713 size:88% position:56% line:0%
was that it actually worked.

00:24:08.746 --> 00:24:10.081 line:0%
My favorite part
was just the whole experience,

00:24:10.114 --> 00:24:12.116 line:0%
doing science,
meeting cool people.

00:24:12.149 --> 00:24:14.819 align:start position:0% line:0%
It's just a really good
simulation of how
the brain works,

00:24:14.852 --> 00:24:17.989 align:start position:0% line:0%
and it was just really cool
to take some information
from that.

00:24:18.022 --> 00:24:19.190
Michael:
And as always,

00:24:19.223 --> 00:24:21.626
thanks for...being a brain.

00:24:21.659 --> 00:24:23.728
( all cheer )

00:24:32.870 --> 00:24:33.805
Michael:
<i> Our small-town brain</i>

00:24:33.838 --> 00:24:35.940
<i> did work as predicted.</i>

00:24:35.973 --> 00:24:38.643
Made up of only 
a couple hundred neurons,

00:24:38.676 --> 00:24:41.646
each one with no idea
what number I was drawing,

00:24:41.679 --> 00:24:43.882
it was nonetheless able
to process the image

00:24:43.915 --> 00:24:45.283
and determine
the correct answer.

00:24:45.316 --> 00:24:48.887
We created a living,
breathing model

00:24:48.920 --> 00:24:50.688
of a part of the human brain.

00:24:50.721 --> 00:24:52.624
And our demonstration
was a new way

00:24:52.657 --> 00:24:54.125
to illustrate and share

00:24:54.158 --> 00:24:56.928
how the human brain
processes visual information.

00:24:56.961 --> 00:25:00.832
We were able to watch it
process...think...

00:25:00.865 --> 00:25:03.268
in real time,
and that's amazing.

00:25:03.301 --> 00:25:06.871
Its success shows what we can
achieve by working together,

00:25:06.904 --> 00:25:10.241
and we only used
a small fraction

00:25:10.274 --> 00:25:13.244
of the number of neurons found
in an actual human brain.

00:25:13.277 --> 00:25:15.213
So imagine how powerful

00:25:15.246 --> 00:25:17.849
the connections of
not a few hundred,

00:25:17.882 --> 00:25:21.586
but a hundred<i> billion </i>
human neurons could be.

00:25:21.619 --> 00:25:23.655
Now, interestingly,

00:25:23.688 --> 00:25:27.258
a hundred billion people
is about how many humans

00:25:27.291 --> 00:25:31.563
have ever existed
in the history of Earth.

00:25:31.596 --> 00:25:34.832
There are only a few billion
alive right now,

00:25:34.865 --> 00:25:39.671
so I guess that means
get procreating...please.

00:25:39.704 --> 00:25:42.807
I want to make a bigger
superhuman mind.

00:25:42.840 --> 00:25:45.343
No. I'd like to thank
every neuron

00:25:45.376 --> 00:25:47.579
from my hometown of Stilwell,

00:25:47.612 --> 00:25:50.014
and the entire community
that supported us.

00:25:50.047 --> 00:25:52.584
Because without them
all working together,

00:25:52.617 --> 00:25:54.986
none of this
would have been possible.

00:25:55.019 --> 00:25:56.754
And as always...

00:25:56.787 --> 00:25:59.090
thanks for watching.

00:25:59.123 --> 00:26:02.293
<i> ♪</i>

00:26:02.326 --> 00:26:05.096 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
- ( no audible dialogue )
-<i> ♪</i>

